{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades and a powerful ruler in the Levant. Born around 1134 Raymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and his wife Countess Maria of Antioch. He inherited the county upon his father's death in 1154.\n\n*Early Reign and Consolidation of Power*\n\nRaymond III's early reign was marked by consolidating his power both internally and externally. He faced challenges from rebellious vassals and external threats from Muslim rulers. He successfully quelled internal dissent and strengthened the county's defenses. He engaged in diplomatic maneuvering maintaining alliances with other Crusader states like the Kingdom of Jerusalem and Antioch. He also actively participated in military campaigns against the Ayyubid dynasty a rising power in the region. Raymond III demonstrated shrewd political skills effectively balancing the interests of his vassals and the needs of the county. He worked to establish a stable and prosperous county fostering trade and economic growth. He invested in infrastructure and promoted the development of Tripoli as a major commercial center.\n\nRaymond III was a key participant in the Third Crusade. He provided crucial military support to King Richard the Lionheart and other Crusader forces. He played a significant role in the siege of Acre a major Crusader stronghold. His military leadership and strategic acumen were highly valued by the Crusaders. After the Third Crusade Raymond focused on rebuilding and strengthening Tripoli. He repaired fortifications and restored infrastructure damaged during the conflict. He continued to engage in diplomatic efforts seeking to maintain a fragile peace with the Ayyubids.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nRaymond III was known for his diplomatic abilities. He cultivated relationships with various powers including the papacy the Holy Roman Empire and Muslim rulers. He sought to maintain a balance of power in the Levant preventing any single entity from dominating the region. His diplomatic efforts often involved strategic alliances and carefully negotiated treaties. He understood the importance of maintaining peaceful relations with Muslim neighbors particularly for the sake of trade and economic stability. He often engaged in correspondence with European rulers seeking support for his county and for the Crusader cause.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\n\nRaymond III died in 1199 after a long and eventful reign. He left behind a county that was relatively stable and prosperous. He was remembered as a skilled military leader a shrewd diplomat and a capable administrator. His reign marked a period of significant growth and development for Tripoli. He successfully defended the county against numerous threats and maintained its independence for many years. His death led to a succession crisis but his legacy as a powerful and influential ruler endured. He is considered one of the most important Counts of Tripoli and a prominent figure in the history of the Crusades. His efforts to maintain peace and stability in the Levant were widely admired.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2371193766593933}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.20648594200611115}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.054002758115530014}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.0002401778765488416}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.8371408916427754e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -7.152531907195225e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.58813522